!!id:"https://tson.io/test-suite/schemas/sidecar-common.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/35/m/meta.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/35/m/core.tn"
@doc:"""
  The declarations every layer's sidecar schema shares. Imported rather than copied: these four sat
  duplicated in all four layer schemas, where a change had to be made four times and a divergence
  was invisible.
  """
{
  @doc:"""
    One of the spec's own four §8.1 diagnostic categories. Written out on an error vector rather
    than inferred from the directory it lives in, so a vector stays self-describing if it moves --
    and because the layer a vector exercises is a processing stage, not a category: the vocabulary
    layer raises `resolver` and `validation` errors, never a `vocabulary` one.
    """
  error_category => !enum [lexer parser resolver validation]

  @doc:"""
    Present only when the subject `.tn` is not plain UTF-8. A runner MUST feed such a subject's
    bytes through unchanged -- reading it into a string first re-encodes it, and for `invalid-utf8`
    the decode substitutes U+FFFD before the lexer sees anything, so the vector would assert against
    a different document than the one on disk. §9.1 permits utf-16/utf-32, so an implementation that
    reads only UTF-8 skips those rather than failing them.
    """
  encoding_name => !enum [invalid-utf8 utf-16 utf-32]

  @doc:"""
    Fields every sidecar carries, whatever its layer or outcome. `spec` names the section the
    vector targets (metadata: not an identifier, and nothing keys on it); `description` says in one
    line what it exercises and why it is interesting.
    """
  sidecar_common => {
    spec: text
    description: text
    encoding: encoding_name?
  }

  @doc:"""
    The payload of an `error` outcome. Position is deliberately absent: implementations legitimately
    fail at different points depending on how far they look ahead, so what is normative is that an
    error of this category occurs, not where. §8.1's MUST is that a processor report a position, not
    that two processors agree on one.
    """
  vector_error => {
    category: error_category
  }

  @doc:"""
    The directives a runner splices into a subject's own header before parsing it, each naming its
    target by a short, unversioned name (`meta.tn`, `core.tn`) rather than the revision-stamped
    identity the directive finally carries. A vector that hardcoded
    `https://tson.io/2026/35/m/core.tn` would have to be edited at every revision bump; naming the
    target here means the runner's own short-name table is the only thing that moves.

    The splice is not a prepend: the header grammar is a fixed sequence, so the directives go in
    immediately after the subject's own `!!id` line, or at the very start where it has none. `meta`
    and `import` govern a schema-document subject; `schema` governs a data-document subject, which
    is what a `class2/validate/` vector needs.
    """
  spliced_header => {
    meta: text?
    import: [text]?
    schema: text?
  }

  @doc:"""
    Which of §8.2's three name-hygiene mechanisms refused a document. They are named rather than
    inferred for the same reason an error vector names its category: the mechanisms are three
    different rules over three different tables, and an implementation that refused a document by
    the wrong one has not passed the vector.
    """
  hygiene_mechanism => !enum [skeleton-distinctness identifier-status restriction-level]

  @doc:"""
    The payload of a `refused` outcome -- §8.1's fifth outcome, which §8.2 says MUST NOT be reported
    in any of the four error categories. The document is not invalid: it is *refused by this
    processor*, under a stated policy, and a conforming processor names the UTS #39 data version in
    the refusal.

    `unicode` is that version, and it is the one thing here that is not about the document. Every
    mechanism reads data the Unicode Consortium declines to freeze -- `confusables.txt`,
    `IdentifierStatus.txt`, the script tables -- so a verdict can change under a routine UCD
    refresh, and §8.2 says two implementations may legitimately disagree with the version the only
    thing that explains it. A vector is therefore labelled with the version it was computed against,
    which is what makes a disagreement diagnosable rather than a mystery.

    Position is absent for the same reason it is absent from `vector_error`, though §8.2 does say
    where a refusal is reported -- at the second occurrence of a colliding pair, in the manner of
    §2.6's duplicate-key diagnostic. What is normative is the refusal, not where a processor
    noticed it.
    """
  vector_refusal => {
    mechanism: hygiene_mechanism
    unicode: text
  }
}
